<div class="article-body">

<p>My lettuce reservoir hit 84F in June. The kitchen was 71F. Hydroponic water temperature had drifted 13 degrees away from the room, and I had a thermometer for the room and none for the bucket, so for two weeks I blamed everything except the water.</p>

<p>First I lost a tray of seedlings to damping off. Then a whole bucket of basil went yellow from the bottom leaves up. Then the roots on my lettuce went from white and fuzzy to tan and slick, and I started reading about chillers that cost more than my entire setup.</p>

<p>The fix was a $12 outlet timer, a $9 thermometer, and a sheet of foil. Here is the number I was missing: 70F. Above it, the water in your reservoir quietly loses its ability to hold oxygen, and it keeps losing it faster the warmer it gets.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">1. What the Numbers Actually Say</h2>

<p>The ideal water temperature for hydroponics is 65 to 72F (18 to 22C), and most leafy greens and herbs sit happily in that band. Not because the water feels nice, but because of a piece of physics called Henry's Law: as water warms, it can dissolve and hold less gas. Oxygen is a gas. So warmer water holds less of it, and your roots live on that oxygen.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> dissolved oxygen is the real variable. Water temperature is just the dial that controls it. That is why every other reading in your reservoir, pH and EC included, gets harder to trust once the water runs warm.</p>

